#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060720} :activity/id #uuid "52ce9d6f-1df9-5165-aee0-b173c7061fb1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051065} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060720 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060946 :tutorial/content "Suppose that some money $P_0$ is deposited into a bank account. We know the bank balance $P$ after $t$ years is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} P &= P_0\\left(1 + \\dfrac r n \\right)^{nt}, \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the annual interest rate, and $n$ is the number of times payments are compounded per year.\n\nNow suppose that we increase the number of compounding periods per year so that compounding occurs *at every instant.* We call this **continuous compounding.** When continuous compounding occurs, we can determine the balance $P$ at time $t$ using the **continuously compounded growth equation**, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} P &= P_0e^{rt}, \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\ne\\approx 2.718\n$$\n\nis Euler's number. We'll go into a little more detail regarding where this formula comes from at the end of the lesson.\n\nFor example, suppose that a company has $\\$450\\,000$ in the bank and that the balance grows at an annual rate of $4.5\\%$, continuously compounded. Then, to calculate the bank balance $5$ years from now, we can use the continuously compounded growth equation.\n\nIn this situation, we have an initial balance\n\n$$\nP_0= 450\\,000\n$$\n\na growth rate of\n\n$$\nr= 4.5\\% = 0.045\n$$\n\nand $t=5$ years. Substituting these values into the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} P &= P_0e^{rt} \\\\[3pt] &= (450\\,000)e^{(0.045)(5)} \\\\[3pt] &= (450\\,000)e^{0.225} \\\\[3pt] &= (450\\,000)(1.252\\,322\\,716) \\\\[3pt] &\\approx 563\\,545. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the balance in $5$ years will be approximately $\\$563\\,545$.\n\nThe rate $r$ in the continuously compounded growth equation does not have to be an annual rate. It can be over any amount of time. The time $t$ inherits its unit from the rate." :tutorial/id #uuid "72601328-d52f-5d63-b3a2-6ef43ce652ce" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3396 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6b989daa-5d39-5b22-bea5-32e31b65a05c" :step/math-academy-id 18991 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060723}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060721 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059796 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059595} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c9581f5-67cb-5752-8a45-b84ba4f3577e" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680803}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676352} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676343}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Initial Amount"} :step/id #uuid "a12841c7-3f0f-5f1b-8887-a1e874d788b6" :step/math-academy-id 18993 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060722}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060722 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059797 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059596} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c0a7b30-b6b4-584e-9816-506f8ec00813" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050649}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680803}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676461} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676460}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate or Time Duration"} :step/id #uuid "a8f63f8b-ad4e-5714-b3a9-cba55c622104" :step/math-academy-id 18994 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060724}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060723 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059798 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059597} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dcfc681-0632-57a5-bd1e-e03d91d13f37" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049643}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680803}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676388} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676383}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Final Amount After Continuous Compounding Is Applied"} :step/id #uuid "cc3a46ca-0e13-5990-951f-14f1a3df9bd1" :step/math-academy-id 19095 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060721}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060724 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060947 :tutorial/content "The formula for continuously compounded growth, given by\n\n$$\nP=P_0 e^{rt}\n$$\n\nis actually equal to the compound interest equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} P &= P_0 \\left(1 + \\dfrac r n \\right)^{nt}, \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor extremely large $n$ (in other words, as $n \\rightarrow \\infty$).\n\nTo demonstrate, let's take the case of $P_0=1$, $r=1$, and $t=1$. Then the formula for continuously compounded growth gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP &= P_{0}e^{1 \\cdot 1} \\\\\n&= 1e^{1 \\cdot 1} \\\\\n&= e \\\\\n&= 2.7182818…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the compound interest equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP &= P_{0}(1 + \\frac{r}{n})^{nt} \\\\\n&= 1(1 + \\frac{1}{n})^{n \\cdot 1} \\\\\n&= (1 + \\frac{1}{n})^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute a large value of $n$, say\n\n$$\nn=1\\,000\\,000\n$$\n\nthen we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP &= (1 + \\frac{1}{1000000})^{1000000} \\\\\n&= (1.000001)^{1000000} \\\\\n&\\approx 2.718280 \\\\\n&\\approx e\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result matches up nicely with our result from the formula for continuously compounded growth!\n\nHere, we demonstrated that the result holds for the special case where $P_0=r=t=1$. However, it is possible to show that the formula is always true." :tutorial/id #uuid "9199edc0-173e-5919-a918-157af0b90048" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3408 :tutorial/title "The Relationship Between Continuous Compounding and the Compound Interest Equation"} :step/id #uuid "1573837f-ce2f-57ff-a36b-4e12743a9d2c" :step/math-academy-id 19150}] :activity/title "Continuously Compounded Interest" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059916}]}
